Target audience. Delhi Metro commuters planning a single origin-to-destination journey for a chosen local date and departure time, who need a bounded, locally sourced indication of predicted station-pair crowding and, only for highly crowded predicted journeys, an in-app suggestion to leave earlier. The product is also used locally by the people maintaining the Delhi Metro station list, timetable patterns, and public-holiday dates that determine those commuter predictions; the Planning View is a local operating context rather than a protected role.

Complexity hooks
Station names are required and unique after leading and trailing whitespace is ignored; stations that are referenced by timetable patterns must remain available and cannot be removed.
Each timetable pattern must use distinct existing origin and destination stations, a closed day-type value, an inclusive start and end time with the start earlier than the end, and a closed baseline crowding value.
For the same origin, destination, and day type, timetable patterns must never have overlapping inclusive time ranges, so a valid journey can resolve to at most one applicable pattern.
Holiday dates take precedence over ordinary weekday/weekend classification, while non-holiday Saturdays and Sundays are Weekend and all other non-holiday dates are Weekday.
Journey predictions must derive the date classification before matching the station pair and inclusive departure-time range, and must clearly distinguish a valid no-match outcome from invalid planner input.
Only matched predictions become saved historical results; saved journey facts, derived day type, matched crowding, and alert status must remain unchanged after later source-record corrections.
The leave-earlier advisory is strictly limited to High matched crowding, must retain the entered planned time, and must calculate a suggestion exactly 20 minutes earlier even when that crosses into the preceding calendar day.
Prediction history must support exact crowding-level filtering and deterministic ordering by journey date, then planned departure time, then creation order, with clear empty outcomes for both no history and no filter matches.
Invalid edits to stations, holidays, or timetable patterns must preserve the prior saved record and visibly explain why the correction was rejected.】【。
The product must remain understandable and usable before setup data exists, using empty states and preventing actions that depend on unavailable station records.
Evaluation criteria and success metrics
Criterion: Station setup enforces normalized uniqueness and protects referenced stations. Scenario: Create a station named “Rajiv Chowk”, then attempt to create “ Rajiv Chowk ”; the second save is rejected with a visible validation message. After a valid timetable pattern references Rajiv Chowk, an attempt to remove it is prevented, and the station remains available.
Criterion: Timetable setup accepts only valid, non-ambiguous patterns. Scenario: With two saved stations, save a Weekday Rajiv Chowk-to-Kashmere Gate pattern from 08:00 through 09:00. An attempt to save another Weekday pattern for the same pair from 09:00 through 10:00 is rejected because inclusive ranges overlap; attempts with identical endpoints or a start time not earlier than the end time are also visibly rejected.
Criterion: Holiday precedence and ordinary day-type classification are applied correctly. Scenario: Save a holiday for a Saturday with a display name, then request a prediction for that date using matching Holiday and Weekend patterns; the displayed day type is Holiday and the Holiday pattern determines the outcome. For a non-holiday Sunday, the displayed day type is Weekend.
Criterion: A matched planner request produces the defined prediction facts and historical result. Scenario: With a valid matching pattern for the selected station pair, derived day type, and departure time, submit a journey; the result shows the submitted origin, destination, date, planned departure time, derived day type, and the pattern’s baseline crowding, and it appears in saved prediction history.
Criterion: Invalid and unmatched journey requests are differentiated. Scenario: Submit a planner request with the same origin and destination or with required fields omitted; the relevant invalid inputs are visibly identified and no result is saved. Submit valid distinct stations, date, and time for which no applicable pattern exists; the product shows “No prediction available,” saves no result, and shows no leave-earlier alert.
Criterion: High-crowding advice is exact and bounded. Scenario: Submit a matching High journey at 00:10; the saved result displays “Leave 20 minutes earlier” and a suggested departure time of 23:50 on the preceding calendar day while retaining 00:10 as the planned time. Equivalent Low and Medium matches show no leave-earlier alert.
Criterion: Source corrections affect future predictions but not historical snapshots. Scenario: Save a matched prediction, then validly correct the matching timetable pattern’s crowding level and submit the same journey again; the new prediction uses the corrected level while the earlier saved result retains its original crowding, derived day type, and alert status. An invalid attempted correction leaves the source record unchanged and shows its validation reason.
Criterion: Prediction history filtering, ordering, and empty states are observable. Scenario: With saved results across dates, times, and crowding levels, open the unfiltered list and verify ascending journey date and planned departure time ordering, with same-time ties in creation order. Select High and verify only High results appear; select a level with no matches and verify a visible no-results explanation. With no saved results, verify the list explains that no journey predictions have been created.
